WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 457: Episode 457: Pope Leo I (The Great): The Confrontation at the Mincio River (452)

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:11.120
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories, where we uncover the real, well-sourced, and lesser-known tales behind history's most prominent figures.

2
00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:12.880
[Rebecca] I'm your host, Rebecca.

3
00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:16.640
[Rebecca] Tonight we begin a landmark series, The Popes of History.

4
00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:28.240
[Rebecca] Across 2,000 years, the Bishop of Rome has stood at the pivot of world history, not merely as a spiritual leader, but as a statesman, diplomat, and defender when empires collapsed around him.

5
00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:33.680
[Rebecca] Tonight, we journey back to the 5th century, to the dying days of the Western Roman Empire.

6
00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:40.080
[Rebecca] The barbarian hordes of Attila the Hun are ravaging Italy, slaughtering populations, and marching toward Rome.

7
00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:43.440
[Rebecca] The Emperor has fled, the Roman army has dissolved.

8
00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:51.760
[Rebecca] Step into the shoes of Pope Leo I, an unarmed 60-year-old priest who rode out to face the most ruthless barbarian warrior on earth.

9
00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:56.480
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story.

10
00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:16.080
[George] For decades, the Roman Empire had been rotting from within, hemorrhaging provinces to Germanic tribes, but nothing in the memory of the Mediterranean world compared to the nightmare advancing from the north.

11
00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:24.480
[George] His name was Attila, king of the Huns, known to terrified European chroniclers as the Flagellum Dei, the Scourge of God.

12
00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:37.360
[George] Leading a savage, multi-ethnic coalition of Hunnic horse archers, Ostrogoths, and Jeppids, Attila had spent the previous year ravaging Gaul before being checked at the bloody stalemate of the Catalonian plains.

13
00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:47.440
[George] Now, hungry for vengeance and desperate for Italian gold, Attila led his mounted host over the Julian Alps and descended upon the fertile plains of northern Italy.

14
00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:54.320
[George] His first target was Aquileia, the heavily fortified Roman fortress city that guarded the eastern entrance to Italy.

15
00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:59.160
[George] When Aquileia resisted, Attila laid siege for three grueling months.

16
00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:06.120
[George] When the walls finally crumbled beneath Hunnic battering rams, Attila ordered a massacre of biblical proportions.

17
00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:17.160
[George] The city was systematically sacked, burned, and leveled so completely that within a generation, local travelers could barely identify the site where the great metropolis had stood.

18
00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:30.360
[George] Survivors fled in sheer terror into the muddy lagoons of the Adriatic Sea, driving wooden piles into the marshy islands to build primitive huts, an act of desperate refuge that would ultimately give birth to the city of Venice.

19
00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:35.720
[George] From the ruins of Aquileia, Attila's horsemen swept westward across the Po valley.

20
00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:39.240
[George] Padua, Vicenza and Verona were put to the torch.

21
00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:47.080
[George] When the Huns reached Milan, the glittering former imperial capital, the citizens surrendered without a fight, begging for mercy.

22
00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:58.600
[George] Attila took up residence in the royal palace, where he noticed a famous Roman fresco depicting Roman emperors seated upon golden thrones while barbarian princes lay prostrated at their feet.

23
00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:04.520
[George] With grim, mocking humor, Attila summoned a local artist and ordered the painting reversed.

24
00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:12.280
[George] Now Attila sat on the throne, while the Roman emperors Valentinian and Theodosius grovelled before him with sacks of tribute.

25
00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:17.240
[George] While northern Italy burned, what was the response of the mighty Roman Empire?

26
00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:19.800
[George] Total, humiliating paralysis.

27
00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:24.840
[George] In Rome and Ravenna, the imperial apparatus collapsed into panicked squabbling.

28
00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:34.920
[George] The Western Roman Emperor was thirty-three-year-old Valentinian III, an arrogant, dissolute ruler utterly devoid of military talent or civic courage.

29
00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:46.000
[George] Terrified that Hunnic outriders would cut off his escape, Valentinian abandoned his palace in Rome and fled behind the tidal marshes and impenetrable canal fortifications of Ravenna.

30
00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:56.160
[George] His chief military commander was Flavius Aetius, the famed last of the Romans, the general who had miraculously halted Attila in Gaul the previous year.

31
00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:00.400
[George] But in 452, Aetius was completely empty-handed.

32
00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:03.680
[George] His veteran Roman legions had been shattered in Gaul.

33
00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:14.240
[George] His Germanic barbarian allies, the Visigoths and Burgundians, flatly refused to cross the Alps to defend Italy, preferring to protect their own newly carved kingdoms.

34
00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:22.800
[George] Aetius commanded only a few thousand garrison troops, hopelessly insufficient to meet Attila's hundred thousand strong host in open battle.

35
00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.760
[George] The Roman Senate met in emergency session amidst whispers of impending doom.

36
00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:42.560
[George] Rumors flew through the Roman forum that Attila planned to cross the Apennine Mountains, surround Rome, starve its hundreds of thousands of inhabitants, and reduce the cradle of Western civilization to a smoking crater just as he had done to Aquileia.

37
00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:49.200
[George] Wealthy patricians loaded their wagons with silver plate and fled south towards Sicily and Constantinople.

38
00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:55.120
[George] The legions had failed, the imperial throne had fled, the sword of Rome was broken.

39
00:04:55.120 --> 00:05:05.840
[George] It was in this vacuum of utter despair that the citizens of Rome turned their eyes away from the imperial palace on the Palatine Hill and looked across the Tiber toward the Vatican and the Lateran.

40
00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:07.840
[George] The man who sat upon the chair of St.

41
00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:16.560
[George] Peter was sixty-two-year-old Leo, a Roman aristocrat of towering intellect, iron discipline, and profound theological conviction.

42
00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:28.880
[George] Elected bishop of Rome in 440, Leo had already earned the historical epithet, the Great by reforming the Western Church, suppressing heresies, and championing the primacy of the Roman See.

43
00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:37.400
[George] To Leo, Rome was not merely an imperial capital, it was the sacred city consecrated by the blood of the apostles Peter and Paul.

44
00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:44.760
[George] Where pagan emperors saw political boundaries, Leo saw a spiritual commonwealth that no barbarian horde could conquer.

45
00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:53.000
[George] As the Roman Senate debated total surrender or mass suicide, Leo stepped into the Curia with calm, commanding authority.

46
00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:57.160
[George] He proposed an audacious, near suicidal diplomatic gambit.

47
00:05:57.160 --> 00:06:06.760
[George] Rather than waiting for Attila to bring fire and slaughter to the gates of Rome, an official imperial embassy would ride north directly into the Hunnic camp to negotiate.

48
00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:15.080
[George] When the terrified senators hesitated to volunteer for what seemed like a guaranteed execution, Leo offered to lead the embassy himself.

49
00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:30.440
[George] Shamed by the Pope's fearless resolve, two high-ranking Roman civic officials agreed to accompany him Genadius Avianus, a former consul and one of the wealthiest patricians in Rome, and Trigetius, the former Praetorian Prefect of Italy.

50
00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:35.480
[George] The mission was armed with neither swords, shields, nor armed escorts.

51
00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:44.040
[George] They carried only diplomatic mandates from the Senate, rich gifts of gold from the imperial treasury, and the moral authority of the Roman Church.

52
00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:57.160
[George] In June 452, Pope Leo and his small retinue mounted their horses and set out from Rome, riding northward along the Via Flaminia through landscapes choked with refugees and blackened by war.

53
00:06:57.160 --> 00:07:01.160
[George] The journey was long, exhausting, and fraught with peril.

54
00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:06.920
[George] As Leo and the ambassadors rode through central Italy, the summer heat became suffocating.

55
00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:15.840
[George] The fields lay unharvested, famine was spreading rapidly, and outbreaks of malaria and dysentery were decimating villages along the route.

56
00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:22.080
[George] In late June, after weeks of hard riding, the papal embassy reached the region of Mantua in Lombardy.

57
00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:30.720
[George] There, where the river Mincio flows out from Lake Garda before emptying into the Po, Attila had established his central command encampment.

58
00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:33.520
[George] It was an astonishing, terrifying sight.

59
00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:41.840
[George] Tens of thousands of Hunnic felt yurts, barbarian wagons, and horse corrals spread across the green plains as far as the eye could see.

60
00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:52.640
[George] The air was thick with the smoke of roasting meat, the neighing of tens of thousands of steppe ponies, and the guttural shouts of warriors sharpening their battle axes and composite bows.

61
00:07:52.640 --> 00:08:03.520
[George] As the small Roman delegation approached the Hunnic pickets, horsemen in iron scale armor and wolf pelt cloaks circled them at a gallop, brandishing javelins and barking orders.

62
00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:06.080
[George] Leo remained completely composed.

63
00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:15.600
[George] Refusing to show a glimmer of fear, the Pope rode tall in the saddle, wearing his white liturgical vestments and holding a plain wooden pastoral cross.

64
00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:23.280
[George] The Hunnic guards escorted the three Roman envoys through the heart of the roaring encampment toward the royal pavilion of the King of Kings.

65
00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:28.480
[George] Attila awaited them, surrounded by his fierce generals and shamanic diviners.

66
00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:41.040
[George] Attila was a short, broad chested man with a large head, small piercing eyes, a thin greying beard, and a flat nose, features that exuded an aura of supreme, unshakable command.

67
00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:47.520
[George] He was accustomed to seeing Roman ambassadors tremble, weep, and grovel on their knees for their lives.

68
00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:58.400
[George] But as Pope Leo stepped into the tent, the barbarian king encountered something he had never witnessed in all his conquests, a man who looked him straight in the eyes without a trace of fear.

69
00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:09.160
[George] What transpired between the Scourge of God and the Bishop of Rome on the banks of the River Mincio remains one of the most mesmerizing dramatic encounters in the annals of Western civilization.

70
00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:16.760
[George] Because no stenographer recorded their exact words, history and legend have woven together an unforgettable tapestry.

71
00:09:16.760 --> 00:09:29.160
[George] According to contemporary 5th century chronicler Prosper of Aquitaine, who served as Leo's personal secretary, the Pope addressed the Hunnic warlord with astonishing eloquence and psychological mastery.

72
00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:33.720
[George] Leo did not grovel, nor did he issue foolish military threats.

73
00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:41.080
[George] Instead, he appealed to Attila's sense of destiny, his pride, and his superstitious dread of divine vengeance.

74
00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:46.920
[George] Leo reminded Attila of what had happened forty years earlier to Alaric, king of the Visigoths.

75
00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:52.920
[George] In 410, Alaric had committed the ultimate sacrilege by capturing and sacking Rome.

76
00:09:52.920 --> 00:10:02.760
[George] Within months of leaving the city, Alaric was struck down by a mysterious sudden illness, dying in the prime of his life and being buried in the bed of the Busento River.

77
00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:05.560
[George] Did Attila wish to suffer the same fate?

78
00:10:05.560 --> 00:10:11.640
[George] Did he wish to conquer the earthly capital of the world only to invite the wrath of the living God upon his head?

79
00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:15.720
[George] Furthermore, Leo possessed sharp geopolitical intelligence.

80
00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:21.000
[George] He knew that behind Attila's fearsome facade, the Hunnic army was in deep trouble.

81
00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:28.440
[George] Italy was suffering a catastrophic drought, and the Huns were running out of grain and forage for their hundreds of thousands of horses.

82
00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:36.040
[George] Worse still, a deadly epidemic of dysentery was ripping through the Hunnic camps, killing thousands of soldiers daily.

83
00:10:36.040 --> 00:10:41.720
[George] Beyond that, messengers from the Danube frontier had just reached Attila with alarming news.

84
00:10:41.720 --> 00:10:50.400
[George] The new Eastern Roman Emperor Martian had launched an offensive across the Danube, attacking the Hunnic homeland while Attila was bogged down in Italy.

85
00:10:50.400 --> 00:11:04.800
[George] Leo offered Attila an honorable, face-saving exit, an immediate, massive cash indemnity from the Roman treasury, an annual tribute, and the eternal prestige of being the conqueror who graciously spared the ancient capital of the world.

86
00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:07.440
[George] Attila listened in absolute silence.

87
00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:17.600
[George] His Hunnic shamans had cast the shoulder blades of sheep into the fire, reading ominous portents in the cracked bones, warning their king that invading Rome would bring death.

88
00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:22.400
[George] Yet it was not mere superstition or strategy that swayed the barbarian king.

89
00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:30.480
[George] Medieval chroniclers record an extraordinary miraculous tradition that circulated through both Roman and Hunnic camps within days of the meeting.

90
00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:41.520
[George] According to the legend later immortalized by Raphael in his Vatican frescoes, while Pope Leo was speaking, Attila looked above the pontiff's head and was struck with sudden terror.

91
00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:52.320
[George] Floating in the air on either side of the Pope, Attila beheld two venerable, majestic figures of towering stature, dressed in apostolic robes and brandishing flaming swords.

92
00:11:52.320 --> 00:12:03.840
[George] They were the apostles Peter and Paul, the patron saints of Rome, silently warning the barbarian monarch that if he took one step further toward their city, they would strike him down on the spot.

93
00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:15.760
[George] Whether one views this as a literal heavenly vision, a psychological hallucination born of a guilty conscience, or a symbolic dramatization of moral authority, the outcome was undeniable.

94
00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:22.240
[George] Attila stood up from his seat, embraced the proposal of peace, and ordered his heralds to sound the retreat.

95
00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:31.400
[George] The Hunnic commanders were stunned, without a drop of Roman blood shed in battle, the scourge of God had agreed to spare Italy and withdraw across the Alps.

96
00:12:31.640 --> 00:12:41.480
[George] In July 452, the vast Hunnic host packed up their yurts, mounted their horses, and began the long trek back toward the Hungarian plains.

97
00:12:41.480 --> 00:12:43.080
[George] Italy was saved.

98
00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:58.600
[George] When Pope Leo and his ambassadors returned to Rome, they were greeted by tens of thousands of cheering citizens who lined the Via Flaminia with olive branches, hailing Leo not merely as their bishop, but as the defensor Civitades, the defender of the city.

99
00:12:58.600 --> 00:13:06.760
[George] The imperial court in Ravenna was humiliated, while the moral and political authority of the Bishop of Rome surged to unprecedented heights.

100
00:13:06.760 --> 00:13:11.880
[George] For Attila, the encounter at the Mincio marked the sudden end of his reign of terror.

101
00:13:11.880 --> 00:13:22.440
[George] In early 453, less than a year after meeting Pope Leo, the Hunnic king celebrated a lavish wedding banquet with a young Gothic noblewoman named Ildico.

102
00:13:22.440 --> 00:13:27.880
[George] Drunk on heavy wine and exhausted from revelry, Attila retired to his bedchamber.

103
00:13:27.880 --> 00:13:35.400
[George] During the night, he suffered a massive nasal hemorrhage, choked on his own blood, and was found dead the following morning.

104
00:13:35.400 --> 00:13:47.560
[George] With Attila's sudden death, his vast empire dissolved into civil war between his sons, and within a few short years, the Hunnic menace vanished from European history like a passing storm.

105
00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:15.000
[Rebecca] Tune in next time as we follow Pope Leo I through his next great trial.

106
00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:20.000
[Rebecca] When the barbarian vandals land at Ostia and the sacking of Rome begins.

107
00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:22.720
[Rebecca] Until next time, I'm Rebecca.

